// `#[column(name: "start_date")]` on a struct field, as far as `meta` -- the attribute parses,
// survives reification and reaches a generator with its argument.
//
// **This is what settled docs/design/meta.md §13's deferred decision.** `Field.attrs` was
// `[string]`, bare names, and said an attribute with an argument "is not modelled yet, and will be
// when one exists rather than guessed at now". One exists, so attributes carry arguments now --
// `attrs: [Attr]` with `Attr { name, args: [AttrArg] }` -- and `has_attr` still reads the same for
// a generator that only asks whether `#[pk]` is there.
//
// `derive(Reflect)` prints what it was handed and generates nothing, which is why it is the
// fixture for a boundary question: anything lost between the parser and a generator shows here.
#[derive(Reflect)]
struct Event {
    #[pk]
    id: int
    #[column(name: "start_date")]
    start: string
    // Two attributes on one field, and the order they are written is the order they arrive.
    #[pk]
    #[column(name: "tenant")]
    owner: int
    note: string
}

fn main() {
    print("reflected")
}
